00:00:01.750 --> 00:00:03.750 align:start position:0%
there's been a lot of press coverage  about people quitting their jobs  well today's report tells you why  here president biden is referring to the  labor department's monthly jobs report a  key gauge of american employment and  inflation the report can move trillions  of dollars in market trades and  influence policy decisions like interest  rates at the federal reserve but the  report still has its limits there are  some things that the drop support just  doesn't include and because of that  we're not getting a complete picture of  the us jobs market  here's what you need to know to read the  monthly jobs report including what it  tells you and what it doesn't  the report which is normally released on  the first friday of the month gives a  look at the current state of the economy  and while economists read it in depth  there are four key areas that they tend  to focus on  non-farm payrolls often simply referred  to as jobs shows the number of positions  added or lost each month that aren't in  agriculture  this chart shows non-farm payrolls over  the past decade you can see job numbers  had been steadily rising until the  pandemic hit when they plummeted  and although jobs had started to recover  by late 2021 job growth had slowed weak  job growth can be a reflection of two  different things  one is that businesses are struggling to  find workers which is largely what we  saw in 2021 sarah cheney kanban reports  on the american labor market for the  wall street journal the other is that  demand for their goods and services is  weak and therefore they're laying off  workers or not hiring  this fluctuating jobs number is directly  tied to the unemployment rate which  measures how many workers in the labor  force are unemployed and currently  looking for work the goal is full  employment which some economists say is  around a four percent to five percent  unemployment rate full employment is  basically  when anyone who wants a job can get a  job when the unemployment rate is really  low  it's  generally a good thing it means that  businesses are not laying off workers  and wages are probably growing pretty  strongly as a result of low joblessness  but if unemployment drops too low it can  signal challenges the downside of low  unemployment can be that companies are  really struggling to find workers and  that can lead to them  raising wages and therefore prices and  you could get very big inflationary  pressures when the unemployment rate  goes down economists look into how much  of that is due to workers getting jobs  and how much is due to shrinking labor  force participation which is measured by  the labor force participation rate or  how much of the overall population is  either currently working or looking for  a job you can see labor force  participation has steadily declined over  the past two decades with the largest  drop happening at the beginning of the  pandemic and when the rate drops too low  it can put pressure on employers  generally when the labor force  participation rate is low as it's been  throughout the pandemic  you might see stronger wage growth and  that's because businesses just can't  find the workers to fill their jobs  they're oftentimes facing what we call  labor shortages and as a result they  ramp up pay  increasing or decreasing pay is measured  by wage growth  look at this chart you'll notice that  wages picked up in 2021 due to labor  shortages and economists expect that  wages will continue to rise  metrics like wage growth give economists  a sense of how the economy is doing but  the data only shows part of the picture  that's because the report is mainly just  hard data which means it can tell us  whether job numbers and wages are going  up or down but it doesn't explain why it  doesn't give us details on how good a  given job is it doesn't tell us how safe  a manufacturing job is or how well an  employer is treating their employees  and there's also the timing of the  report it's based on two core surveys  from the labor department  one of household populations and another  of employment data that take place  around the 12th of every month and as a  result the jobs report primarily  reflects the labor market over these  first two weeks which means there's a  delay in the data  the employment survey collects data from  around 145 000 employers some of whom  may respond to the survey late or not at  all before the pandemic hit  businesses were open and so they were  able to provide responses to the labor  department survey after the pandemic  hits a lot of businesses shut down  either because consumers stopped going  to ex-restaurants or ex-business or  because of government mandated  restrictions if businesses don't respond  the labor department has to guess their  payroll size which can get tricky and  this is what we saw happening during the  pandemic the labor department had a  smaller sample size of businesses to  work with for its payroll survey and as  a result the margin of error when  they're making calculations was higher  this margin of error is important in  part because of just how big the labor  market is in december 2021 it stood at  almost 150 million jobs if the number of  jobs added or lost are tens of thousands  higher or lower than what the report  shows it could make policymakers jobs  harder the federal reserve is really  closely watching those job growth and  payrolls growth numbers and if there's a  big margin of error they might have a  slight misunderstanding what's going on  still the fed looks at multiple data  reports about the economy and over time  estimates from the jobs report that are  too high or low tend to even out so the  implications are shorter term but  despite the pitfalls the jobs report is  still one of the most influential  indicators we have for the american  workforce and economy
 

